    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "interlocking sounds good but a bit of a headache for the guys on the floor. if we stick to column stacking to keep it simple, what are the specific risks we need to watch out for regarding the center of gravity? especially if the loads aren't perfectly uniform.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Column stacking, while simpler, presents higher risks regarding the center of gravity, as imbalanced loads can lead to shifts in weight, potentially causing instability. When dealing with uneven weight distributions, it's vital to take extra care in positioning the pallets. To minimize risks, maintain consistent spacing between columns and avoid having too narrow aisles. With non-uniform loads, ensure that heavier spots are balanced with lighter blocks where possible. Repeating stacks should also be rotated to avoid degrading the racking support beams over time due to recurring heavy points. Regular inspections and pallet adjustments are key to retaining the integrity and safety of the stack. Remember, safety protocols must be stringently adhered to, including using forkside guides or restraints to minimize lifting strain on your personnel. Safety should always be your primary concern in these scenarios.",
